<p>Actual angles depend on material properties, belt speed, and cleat height. Consult manufacturer specifications for your application. See our <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for pricing factors.</p>
<h2 id="food-heading">Food-Grade Cleated Belts</h2>
<p>Food processing requires cleated belts that meet FDA 21 CFR 177 and USDA standards for direct food contact. Food-grade cleated belts use polyurethane (PU) or silicone belt materials instead of rubber. These materials resist oils, acids, and cleaning chemicals while remaining non-toxic and non-absorbent.</p>
<h3>Welded vs. Glued Cleats</h3>
<p>Food-grade cleats are thermally welded (heat-sealed) to the belt surface, not glued. Welded cleats create a monolithic bond with no crevice between the cleat base and belt. Glued cleats create a seam where bacteria, moisture, and food particles accumulate. Every major <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary conveyor</a> specification requires welded cleats. Dorner, Intralox, and Volta Belting produce thermally welded food-grade cleated belts.</p>
<h3>Detection-Friendly Materials</h3>
<p>Blue-pigmented belts are standard in food processing because blue is rare in natural foods, making belt fragments visually detectable if a belt degrades. Metal-detectable belt compounds contain ferrous particles that trigger <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">metal detection and reject systems</a> downstream. X-ray-detectable compounds provide an alternative for facilities using X-ray inspection instead of metal detectors.</p>
<h3>Sanitary Frame Design</h3>
<p>The belt is only part of the sanitary equation. Food-grade cleated conveyors need <a href="/conveyor-types/stainless-steel-conveyors/">stainless steel frames</a> (304 or 316 grade), tool-less belt removal for cleaning, sealed bearings, and open-frame construction that eliminates ledges where debris collects. Quick-release belt tensioners allow operators to remove the belt for cleaning in under five minutes.</p>
<h2 id="sidewall-heading">Sidewall Belt Conveyors</h2>
<p>Sidewall belt conveyors combine corrugated flexible sidewalls with cross-cleats to carry bulk materials at angles from 60 to 90 degrees. The sidewalls prevent edge spillage. The cross-cleats create pockets that contain material against gravity. Together, they form a continuous elevator that replaces <a href="/conveyor-types/bucket-elevators/">bucket elevators</a> in many applications.</p>
<p>Sidewall belts use a base belt with corrugated rubber or polyurethane walls bonded along both edges. The walls flex as the belt wraps around pulleys, then spring back to full height on the carrying run. Cleat spacing and height determine pocket volume and capacity. Continental, Fenner Dunlop, and Bando manufacture sidewall belt systems for applications from grain handling to cement production.</p>
<p>Compared to <a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/">vertical reciprocating conveyors</a>, sidewall belt conveyors provide continuous flow rather than batch lifting. Compared to traditional bucket elevators, sidewall belts handle a wider range of material sizes and are easier to clean. The tradeoff is higher belt cost and more complex belt tracking requirements.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">Specifying a Cleated Belt</h2>
<h3>Cleat Height</h3>
<p>Cleat height must exceed the product height for individual items, or contain the required material depth for bulk handling. Standard cleat heights: 1", 1.5", 2", 3", 4", and 6". Taller cleats increase belt stiffness, require larger pulley diameters to maintain belt life, and reduce the belt's ability to wrap tightly around end pulleys.</p>
<h3>Cleat Spacing</h3>
<p>For discrete products (boxes, bags, bottles), set cleat spacing to product length plus 1-2 inches clearance. For bulk materials, spacing determines pocket volume. Closer spacing increases volumetric capacity per belt foot. Wider spacing reduces belt cost and allows easier cleaning. Typical bulk handling spacing: 4" to 12" for fine materials, 12" to 24" for coarse materials.</p>
<h3>Belt Speed</h3>
<p>Cleated belt speeds are generally lower than flat belt speeds because of the impact loading at the feed point and the potential for product bouncing or shifting at high speeds. Typical speeds range from 30 to 150 FPM. Bulk materials on steep inclines run at 50-100 FPM. Packaged goods on moderate inclines run at 80-150 FPM. Higher speeds require smoother transitions and longer infeed zones.</p>
<h3>Transition Zones</h3>
<p>Every cleated incline conveyor needs horizontal transition zones at the bottom (infeed) and top (discharge). The infeed zone lets products settle against the cleat before the incline begins. The discharge zone levels out before products leave the belt. Without adequate transitions, products shift, tumble, or fail to discharge cleanly. Standard transition length: 2-4 times the product length.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Cleated Belt Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Packaging line elevation changes:</strong> Cleated belt conveyors move packages between floor levels, over equipment, and between mezzanines. A <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging line</a> with a filler on the ground floor and a case packer on a mezzanine uses a cleated incline to bridge the height difference without an elevator.</p>
<p><strong>Agricultural products:</strong> Cleated belts convey fruits, vegetables, grains, and nuts on wash lines, grading lines, and packing lines. Chevron cleats prevent round produce from rolling backward. Food-grade polyurethane belts withstand the water, acids, and sanitizers used in produce processing.</p>
<p><strong>Recycling and waste handling:</strong> Cleated belts elevate mixed recyclables, shredded materials, and sorted fractions from ground-level collection to elevated sorting platforms. High-durability rubber belts with heavy-duty cleats resist abrasion from glass, metal, and aggregate.</p>
<p><strong>Parts feeding:</strong> Small manufactured parts (fasteners, stampings, molded components) ride in cleat pockets to orientation stations, counters, and assembly machines. Cleated belt feeders replace <a href="/conveyor-types/vibratory-conveyors/">vibratory bowl feeders</a> for parts that are too large, too heavy, or too fragile for vibratory feeding.</p>
<p><strong>Bulk material elevating:</strong> Sidewall cleated belts replace bucket elevators for sand, gravel, cement, grain, and animal feed. They handle higher throughput than bucket elevators and accommodate larger particle sizes. Discharge occurs at any point along the belt route, not just at the top.</p>
<p><strong>Chip and scrap handling:</strong> Metal chips, turnings, and stamping scrap ride cleated belts from machining centers to chip bins. For heavier metalworking scrap, <a href="/conveyor-types/hinged-steel-belt/">hinged steel belt conveyors</a> provide greater durability than rubber cleated belts.</p>
<h2 id="maint-heading">Cleated Belt Maintenance</h2>
<p>Cleated belts require more maintenance attention than flat belts because of the cleats themselves. Key maintenance considerations:</p>
<p><strong>Cleat wear and damage:</strong> Cleats wear from abrasion, impact, and chemical exposure. Inspect cleats weekly for tears, delamination from the belt surface, and excessive height loss. A cleat that has lost more than 25% of its original height no longer retains product reliably at the design incline angle.</p>
<p><strong>Belt tracking:</strong> Cleated belts are stiffer than flat belts and resist tracking adjustment. Improper tracking causes edge wear, cleat damage, and belt drift. V-guided belts use a V-shaped profile on the belt underside that rides in a corresponding groove on the slider bed, providing positive tracking without adjustment.</p>
<p><strong>Cleaning:</strong> Material lodges between cleats and in cleat-to-belt joints. In food applications, cleaning between and behind cleats is critical for sanitation compliance. Tool-less belt removal systems allow operators to pull the belt off the conveyor for full cleaning and inspection. Dorner's AquaPruf and mk North America's CleanLine series feature tool-less belt removal.</p>
<p><strong>Belt replacement:</strong> Cleated belts are typically endless (spliced into a loop) or joined with mechanical fasteners. Vulcanized splices are strongest but require field vulcanizing equipment. Mechanical fasteners allow faster field replacement but create a bump at the splice that affects product stability. Food-grade applications require welded or hot-press splices with no mechanical fasteners.</p>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a cleated belt conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A cleated belt conveyor has raised profiles (cleats) attached to the belt surface at regular intervals. Cleats prevent products from sliding backward on inclines and compartmentalize bulk materials for metered feeding. Profiles include T-cleats, L-cleats, chevron patterns, and bucket-style formations for angles from 30 to 90 degrees.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What angle can a cleated belt conveyor handle?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Standard T-cleats handle 30-45 degrees. Chevron and L-cleats handle 35-50 degrees. Sidewall belts with bucket cleats handle 60-90 degrees (full vertical). A smooth flat belt loses traction above 15-18 degrees. Actual capability depends on product characteristics, belt speed, and cleat height.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">Are cleated belt conveyors food-safe?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Yes. Food-grade cleated belts use FDA-compliant polyurethane or silicone with thermally welded cleats (not glued). Welded cleats eliminate bacterial harborage crevices. Blue belts enable visual fragment detection. Dorner, Intralox, and Volta Belting produce food-grade cleated belts meeting FDA 21 CFR 177 and USDA requirements.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What is the difference between cleated and sidewall belts?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Cleated belts have raised profiles across the width to prevent backsliding. Sidewall belts add corrugated walls along both edges to contain loose materials at steep angles. Combined sidewall-and-cleat belts handle vertical lifts of bulk materials. Sidewall belts cost more but eliminate spillage at extreme incline angles.</p>

</div>
</div>
